File: GradedLieAlgebras.info, Node: lieAlgebra_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmWeights=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, Next: LieAlgebraMap, Prev: lieAlgebra_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSigns=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, Up: Top

lieAlgebra(...,Weights=>...) -- optional argument for lieAlgebra
****************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        L = lieAlgebra(gen,Weights => w)
  * Inputs:
      * gen, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, 
      * w, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of lists of weights or a list of
        positive integers
      * w, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 1, the same weight (1,0) is
        assigned to each generator
  * Outputs:
      * L, an instance of the type *note LieAlgebra: LieAlgebra,, 

Description
===========

This is an option to tell *note lieAlgebra: lieAlgebra, to assign the given
weights to the generators. A weight is a list of integers of a length that is
the same for all generators, see *note degreeLength(LieAlgebra):
degreeLength_lpLieAlgebra_rp,. The first component (also just called the
degree) is positive, see *note firstDegree(LieElement): firstDegree,. If the
option *note LastWeightHomological: LastWeightHomological, is true, then the
last component in a weight is the homological degree, which is non-negative and
less than the first degree. If the option *note LastWeightHomological:
LastWeightHomological, is false, then the program adds a last component 0 to
the existing degrees. When the option is given as a list of integers
$n1,n2,\ldots$, which is not possible when the option *note
LastWeightHomological: LastWeightHomological, is true, then the program defines
the weights for the generators to be $\{n1,0\},\ \{n2,0\},\ldots$. The default
value is 1, which has the effect that all generators have weight $\{1,0\}$.

+------------------------------------------------------+
